# Roles associated with requirements in ML-enabled systems

## Answer (hypothesis)
The two roles with the highest proportion of association with requirements are
**Project Lead / Project Manager** and **Data Scientist**. Reading the only role-by-requirements
variable in the file (`Q8_ML_Addressing_<role>`, a per-role multi-select where `quoted` means the
respondent named that role as addressing requirements-related ML activity), and taking as denominator
only the **194** respondents who actually saw/answered the Q8 block (the 82 rows coded `-77` across all
8 Q8 columns never answered the block, exactly like `-99`/`-66` elsewhere = NA, not "not quoted"):

| rank | role | k | n | share (95% CI, bootstrap B=10,000, seed=42, percentile) |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Project Lead / Project Manager | 99 | 194 | **51.031% [43.814, 58.247]** |
| 2 | Data Scientist | 95 | 194 | **48.969% [41.753, 56.186]** |
| 3 | Business Analyst | 50 | 194 | 25.773% [19.588, 31.959] |

Interpretation: requirements work on ML-enabled systems is concentrated in the project-lead and
data-scientist roles, and both are ~2x the next role (Business Analyst). A paired bootstrap on the same
resamples shows the two leaders are statistically indistinguishable from each other
(diff = 2.06 pp, 95% CI [-7.73, +11.34], p = 0.71) but each is clearly above Business Analyst
(DS vs BA: +23.20 pp, CI [13.40, 32.99], p = 0.0001; PL vs BA: +25.26 pp, CI [15.98, 34.02], p = 0.0001).
So the defensible claim is about the **pair** {Project Lead/PM, Data Scientist}, not about which of the
two is rank 1.

Robustness: the same top-2 appears under all four denominators - all 276 rows (35.87% / 34.42%),
Q8-valid 194 (51.03% / 48.97%), Q8-valid + completed only 188 (51.06% / 49.47%), Q8-valid with >=1
quoted role 173 (57.23% / 54.91%) - with a 16-26 pp gap to rank 3 in every case.

## "Association" disambiguation (explicit)
* "Association with requirements" here = a role is quoted as participating in requirements activity
  (`Q8_ML_Addressing_*`). This is the only role x requirements variable in the dataset.
* `D14_ML_Purpose_Association` is **association-rule learning** (an ML purpose), 11.594% overall
  (32/276) and 8.1-18.2% by respondent role. It has nothing to do with the question and was NOT used.

## Discrepancy with the numbers quoted in the question (49.6% / 61.389%)
Nothing was tuned to reproduce them; no denominator shift, no free-text remapping.
* No reading of this file yields 61.389% for any role, and 49.6% is matched at best approximately by
  Data Scientist (48.969% at n=194; 49.468% at n=188). Implied numerators are non-integer
  (0.496*194 = 96.22, 0.61389*194 = 119.09).
* The quoted CIs are impossible for these data: a half-width of 0.381 pp at p = 0.496 requires
  n ~ 66,161 and 0.434 pp at p = 0.61389 requires n ~ 48,455, while the file has 276 rows (194 valid).
  At n = 194 the honest bootstrap CI is ~ +/-7 pp, essentially identical under percentile, BCa,
  bootstrap-SE and normal methods - the width is a property of the sample size, not of the method.
  Conclusion: the cited percentages/CIs come from a different unit of observation or an external source,
  not from this CSV.

## Open / unverified (do not present as facts)
* **Unconfirmed assumption**: that the Q8 block is about *requirements*. The dataset description says
  "Degree to which ML aspects are addressed by <role>" - the literal word "requirements" is not in the
  file, so "association with requirements" is an interpretation.
* `D4_Role == '0'` (23 rows, 10 of them Completed) is treated as NA, but its meaning is **unknown**.
* Q8 wording for `Others` (19 quotes, free text: Director, Product owner, "Architects, Tech Leads and
  managers", "No one", ...) is kept as one category; keyword mapping of `D4_Role_Others` /
  `Q8_ML_Addressing_Others_Free` is arbitrary and only in `sensitivity_arbitrary_others_mapping.csv`.
* `Status == 'Suspended'` (88 rows) is not treated as invalid per se: 6 suspended respondents did
  answer Q8 and are kept in the base.
